{"qid": "gf-13", "paper_id": "2001.06899", "quotes_by_model": {"?": ["Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the \\mathup{{{t}}} \\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}} rejection factor of ≈4.8 .", "Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the 𝑡𝑡¯ rejection factor of ≈4.8.", "Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the pTmiss selection (pTmiss <40 GeV) efficiencies for Z+jets and Z+HF jets events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in Z+HF jets events, which results in large pTmiss values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for Z+HF jets events by ≈1% at high jet and Z boson pT regions compared to those of Z+jets events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet (Z boson) pT bins where pT >60 (90) GeV .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)\\, GeV ."], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["Missing transverse momentum is used in this analysis to reduce background contributions from t t¯ and single top quark production processes. In contrast to the Z+jets , these processes have a significant amount of missing energy because of undetected neutrinos in top quark decays. The missing transverse momentum vector, p¯Tmiss , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, pTmiss , is required to be less than 40 GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the t t¯ rejection factor of ≈4.8 .", "Missing transverse momentum is used in this analysis to reduce background contributions from $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ and single top quark production processes. In contrast to the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ , these processes have a significant amount of missing energy because of undetected neutrinos in top quark decays. The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ selection ( $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}<40\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ ) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events."]}, "answers_by_model": {"?": ["The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen because semileptonic decays of heavy-flavor (HF) hadrons in Z+HF jets events produce large MET values, leading to lower selection efficiencies for those events compared to Z+jets events. This difference in efficiency (≈1% at high-pT regions) necessitates the cut to account for systematic uncertainties, with an uncertainty of 1.5% applied for high-pT bins.", "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to achieve ~80% signal efficiency while providing a ~4.8x rejection of $t\\overline{t}$ background.", "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to achieve ~80% signal efficiency while providing a ~4.8x rejection of tt̄ background.", "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to reduce background from top quark processes (t t̄ and single top) due to their neutrino-induced missing energy. This cut maintains ~80% signal efficiency while achieving a ~4.8x rejection of top quark backgrounds."], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["The MET cut is used to reduce background contributions from top quark production processes. The magnitude of the missing transverse momentum, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40 GeV.", "The MET cut of 40 GeV is mentioned in the context of missing transverse momentum selection efficiency.", "The MET cut of 40 GeV is used to reduce background contributions from t t¯ and single top quark production processes, which have significant missing energy due to undetected neutrinos in top quark decays. This cut results in a signal efficiency of approximately 80% with a t t¯ rejection factor of approximately 4.8.", "The MET cut of 40 GeV is used to reduce background contributions from top quark production processes. This cut results in a signal efficiency of approximately 80% with a top quark rejection factor of approximately 4.8.", "The text mentions a $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ selection cut of 40 GeV, but does not explain why this value was chosen.", "The text mentions a missing transverse momentum selection efficiency with a cut value of 40 GeV."]}, "verdicts": [], "all_quotes": ["Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the \\mathup{{{t}}} \\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}} rejection factor of ≈4.8 .", "Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the 𝑡𝑡¯ rejection factor of ≈4.8.", "Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the pTmiss selection (pTmiss <40 GeV) efficiencies for Z+jets and Z+HF jets events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in Z+HF jets events, which results in large pTmiss values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for Z+HF jets events by ≈1% at high jet and Z boson pT regions compared to those of Z+jets events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet (Z boson) pT bins where pT >60 (90) GeV .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)\\, GeV .", "Missing transverse momentum is used in this analysis to reduce background contributions from t t¯ and single top quark production processes. In contrast to the Z+jets , these processes have a significant amount of missing energy because of undetected neutrinos in top quark decays. The missing transverse momentum vector, p¯Tmiss , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, pTmiss , is required to be less than 40 GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the t t¯ rejection factor of ≈4.8 .", "Missing transverse momentum is used in this analysis to reduce background contributions from $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ and single top quark production processes. In contrast to the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ , these processes have a significant amount of missing energy because of undetected neutrinos in top quark decays. The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ selection ( $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}<40\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ ) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events."], "quote": "Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ≈80% with the \\mathup{{{t}}} \\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}} rejection factor of ≈4.8 .", "answer": true, "answers": ["The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen because semileptonic decays of heavy-flavor (HF) hadrons in Z+HF jets events produce large MET values, leading to lower selection efficiencies for those events compared to Z+jets events. This difference in efficiency (≈1% at high-pT regions) necessitates the cut to account for systematic uncertainties, with an uncertainty of 1.5% applied for high-pT bins.", "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to achieve ~80% signal efficiency while providing a ~4.8x rejection of $t\\overline{t}$ background.", "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to achieve ~80% signal efficiency while providing a ~4.8x rejection of tt̄ background.", "The 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to reduce background from top quark processes (t t̄ and single top) due to their neutrino-induced missing energy. This cut maintains ~80% signal efficiency while achieving a ~4.8x rejection of top quark backgrounds.", "The MET cut is used to reduce background contributions from top quark production processes. The magnitude of the missing transverse momentum, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40 GeV.", "The MET cut of 40 GeV is mentioned in the context of missing transverse momentum selection efficiency.", "The MET cut of 40 GeV is used to reduce background contributions from t t¯ and single top quark production processes, which have significant missing energy due to undetected neutrinos in top quark decays. This cut results in a signal efficiency of approximately 80% with a t t¯ rejection factor of approximately 4.8.", "The MET cut of 40 GeV is used to reduce background contributions from top quark production processes. This cut results in a signal efficiency of approximately 80% with a top quark rejection factor of approximately 4.8.", "The text mentions a $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ selection cut of 40 GeV, but does not explain why this value was chosen.", "The text mentions a missing transverse momentum selection efficiency with a cut value of 40 GeV."], "quote_supports": true, "support_why": "The sentences explain that the 40 GeV MET cut was chosen to achieve a signal efficiency of ≈80% and a tt̄ rejection factor of ≈4.8. They also link the cut to addressing differences in missing transverse momentum efficiencies between Z+jets and Z+HF jets events, which arise from semileptonic decays of HF hadrons. This directly ties the choice of 40 GeV to the paper's analysis requirements and systematic considerations.", "judged_by": "Qwen/QwQ-32B-AWQ"}
{"qid": "gf-10", "paper_id": "2001.06899", "quotes_by_model": {"?": ["Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "In the above formulas, the integrated luminosity as well as the efficiencies that are related to lepton and p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} requirements in the {\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}} event selection cancel.", "In the above formulas, the integrated luminosity as well as the efficiencies that are related to lepton and p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} requirements in the {\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets} event selection cancel.", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV ."], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ selection ( $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}<40\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ ) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ( $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV ."]}, "answers_by_model": {"?": ["The pT^miss < 40 GeV cut retains a signal efficiency of approximately .8 (80%)", "The pT^miss < 40 GeV cut retains a signal efficiency of approximately 80%"], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["The $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}<40\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ cut has an uncertainty of 1.5% in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ( $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "The missing transverse momentum selection efficiency for the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}<40\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ cut is discussed. The efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ( $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "The pT^miss < 40 GeV cut has an efficiency that is lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ( $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "The pT^miss < 40 GeV cut retains a signal efficiency of approximately 80%."]}, "verdicts": [], "all_quotes": ["Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "Its magnitude, p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "In the above formulas, the integrated luminosity as well as the efficiencies that are related to lepton and p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} requirements in the {\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}} event selection cancel.", "In the above formulas, the integrated luminosity as well as the efficiencies that are related to lepton and p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} requirements in the {\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets} event selection cancel.", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ selection ($p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}<40\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{HF~jets}}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{jets}}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ($\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "The missing transverse momentum vector, ${\\vec{p}}_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\kern 1.0pt\text{miss}}$ , is computed as the negative vector sum of the transverse momenta of all PF candidates in an event [ 41 ] and is further modified to account for corrections to the energy scale of the reconstructed jets. Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "Missing transverse momentum selection efficiency: This uncertainty source accounts for possible differences in the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ selection ( $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}<40\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ ) efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events. The effect comes from contributions of semileptonic decays of HF hadrons in ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events, which results in large $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}$ values. Therefore, the efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ( $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV ."], "quote": "Its magnitude, $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ , is required to be less than 40  GeV , which results in a signal efficiency of ${\\approx}80\\%$ with the $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}}$ rejection factor of ${\\approx}4.8$ .", "answer": true, "answers": ["The $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}<40\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ cut has an uncertainty of 1.5% in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ( $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "The missing transverse momentum selection efficiency for the $p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\text{miss}}<40\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ cut is discussed. The efficiencies tend to be lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ( $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "The pT^miss < 40 GeV cut has an efficiency that is lower for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events by ${\\approx}1\\%$ at high jet and $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ regions compared to those of ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ events. An uncertainty of 1.5% is included in the R(c/j) and R(b/j) differential results for jet ( $\\mathup{{{Z}}}$ boson) $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ bins where $p_{\\mathrm{T}}>60$ (90)  GeV .", "The pT^miss < 40 GeV cut retains a signal efficiency of approximately .8 (80%)", "The pT^miss < 40 GeV cut retains a signal efficiency of approximately 80%", "The pT^miss < 40 GeV cut retains a signal efficiency of approximately 80%."], "quote_supports": true, "support_why": "The cited sentences explicitly state that the p_{\\mathrm{T}}^{\\text{miss}} < 40 GeV cut results in a signal efficiency of ≈80%, directly addressing the question. The sentences provide the exact value sought, even if other parts discuss uncertainties or related contexts.", "judged_by": "Qwen/QwQ-32B-AWQ"}
{"qid": "gf-15", "paper_id": "2006.05880", "quotes_by_model": {"?": ["Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$", "The observed and expected yields in these VRs are shown in Table 5 , with a purity of FNP leptons above 55% and with good agreement between data and the background estimates.", "Table 7: Background fit results for the control and validation regions... The number of events with fake/non-prompt leptons is estimated with the data-driven technique described in Section Fake/non-prompt lepton background .", "The observed and expected yields in these VRs are shown in Table 5...", "Table 7: Background fit results for the control and validation regions for the $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson backgrounds... The number of events with fake/non-prompt leptons is estimated with the data-driven technique described in Section Fake/non-prompt lepton background .", "The normalisation factor for the $t\\bar{t}$ background was found to be $1.09\\pm 0.13$ .", "Table 9 shows the observed and expected yields in the CR for each background source, and Figure 3 shows the distribution of the transverse mass and number of Higgs boson candidtes after the background fit.", "Figure 4 shows the definition, the observed number of events and expected yields in these regions, which feature either the same $b{\\textrm{-tagged\\,jets}}$ multiplicity as the SRs with relaxed $m_{\\textrm{T}}$ requirements, or the same $m_{\\textrm{T}}$ selections as the SRs requiring exactly three $b$ -tagged jets.", "The ‘Higgs’ category contains the contributions from gluon–gluon fusion, vector-boson fusion, $Wh$ , $Zh$ and $t\\bar{t}h$ production.", "The ‘Others’ category contains the contributions from $t\\bar{t}WW$ , $t\\bar{t}WZ$ , $t\\bar{t}t$ and $t\\bar{t}t\\bar{t}$ production.", "Total (post-fit) SM events\n$5.7$\n$\\pm$\n$1.0$\n$12.1$\n$\\pm$\n$2.0$\n$5.6$\n$\\pm$\n$1.6$\n$5.5$\n$\\pm$\n$0.9$"], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 ℓ and 1 ℓ SRs, respectively.", "$S_{\\textrm{exp}}^{95}$ ${6.2}^{+2.6}_{-1.6}$", "Table 10: Observed and expected numbers of events in the 3 ℓ signal regions. The pre-fit predictions from MC simulation are given for comparison for those backgrounds ( t¯tZ , multi-boson) that are normalised to data in dedicated control regions.", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 $\\ell$ and 1 $\\ell$ SRs, respectively.", "Table 10: Observed and expected numbers of events in the 3 $\\ell$ signal regions. The pre-fit predictions from MC simulation are given for comparison for those backgrounds ( $t\bar{t}Z$ , multi-boson) that are normalised to data in dedicated control regions."]}, "answers_by_model": {"?": ["In SR^Z_1A, the observed yield is 3 events. The expected yield (total post-fit SM events) is 5.7 ±1.0.", "The observed yield in SR^Z_1A is 3 events. The expected yield (post-fit SM events) is 5.7 ± 1.0."], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["The observed number of events in SR^Z_1A is 3. The expected total SM events in SR^Z_1A is 5.7 ± 1.0.", "The observed number of events in SR^Z_1A is 3. The expected yield is 5.7 ± 1.0.", "The observed number of events in SR^Z_1A is 3. The expected yields include various components such as multi-boson, $t\\bar{t}Z$, fake or non-prompt leptons, $tZ$, $tWZ$, and others. The total post-fit SM events are 5.7 ± 1.0. The 95% CL upper limits on the visible number of BSM events ($S_{\\textrm{obs}}^{95}$) is 4.5, the number of BSM events given the expected number of background events ($S_{\\textrm{exp}}^{95}$) is ${6.2}^{+2.6}_{-1.6}$, and the visible BSM cross-section ($\\sigma_{\\mathrm{vis}}$) is 0.03 fb. The discovery $p$ -value ($p_{0}$) for the background-only hypothesis is 0.50."]}, "verdicts": [], "all_quotes": ["Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$", "The observed and expected yields in these VRs are shown in Table 5 , with a purity of FNP leptons above 55% and with good agreement between data and the background estimates.", "Table 7: Background fit results for the control and validation regions... The number of events with fake/non-prompt leptons is estimated with the data-driven technique described in Section Fake/non-prompt lepton background .", "The observed and expected yields in these VRs are shown in Table 5...", "Table 7: Background fit results for the control and validation regions for the $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson backgrounds... The number of events with fake/non-prompt leptons is estimated with the data-driven technique described in Section Fake/non-prompt lepton background .", "The normalisation factor for the $t\\bar{t}$ background was found to be $1.09\\pm 0.13$ .", "Table 9 shows the observed and expected yields in the CR for each background source, and Figure 3 shows the distribution of the transverse mass and number of Higgs boson candidtes after the background fit.", "Figure 4 shows the definition, the observed number of events and expected yields in these regions, which feature either the same $b{\\textrm{-tagged\\,jets}}$ multiplicity as the SRs with relaxed $m_{\\textrm{T}}$ requirements, or the same $m_{\\textrm{T}}$ selections as the SRs requiring exactly three $b$ -tagged jets.", "The ‘Higgs’ category contains the contributions from gluon–gluon fusion, vector-boson fusion, $Wh$ , $Zh$ and $t\\bar{t}h$ production.", "The ‘Others’ category contains the contributions from $t\\bar{t}WW$ , $t\\bar{t}WZ$ , $t\\bar{t}t$ and $t\\bar{t}t\\bar{t}$ production.", "Total (post-fit) SM events\n$5.7$\n$\\pm$\n$1.0$\n$12.1$\n$\\pm$\n$2.0$\n$5.6$\n$\\pm$\n$1.6$\n$5.5$\n$\\pm$\n$0.9$", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 ℓ and 1 ℓ SRs, respectively.", "$S_{\\textrm{exp}}^{95}$ ${6.2}^{+2.6}_{-1.6}$", "Table 10: Observed and expected numbers of events in the 3 ℓ signal regions. The pre-fit predictions from MC simulation are given for comparison for those backgrounds ( t¯tZ , multi-boson) that are normalised to data in dedicated control regions.", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 $\\ell$ and 1 $\\ell$ SRs, respectively.", "Table 10: Observed and expected numbers of events in the 3 $\\ell$ signal regions. The pre-fit predictions from MC simulation are given for comparison for those backgrounds ( $t\bar{t}Z$ , multi-boson) that are normalised to data in dedicated control regions."], "quote": "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$", "answer": true, "answers": ["In SR^Z_1A, the observed yield is 3 events. The expected yield (total post-fit SM events) is 5.7 ±1.0.", "The observed number of events in SR^Z_1A is 3. The expected total SM events in SR^Z_1A is 5.7 ± 1.0.", "The observed number of events in SR^Z_1A is 3. The expected yield is 5.7 ± 1.0.", "The observed number of events in SR^Z_1A is 3. The expected yields include various components such as multi-boson, $t\\bar{t}Z$, fake or non-prompt leptons, $tZ$, $tWZ$, and others. The total post-fit SM events are 5.7 ± 1.0. The 95% CL upper limits on the visible number of BSM events ($S_{\\textrm{obs}}^{95}$) is 4.5, the number of BSM events given the expected number of background events ($S_{\\textrm{exp}}^{95}$) is ${6.2}^{+2.6}_{-1.6}$, and the visible BSM cross-section ($\\sigma_{\\mathrm{vis}}$) is 0.03 fb. The discovery $p$ -value ($p_{0}$) for the background-only hypothesis is 0.50.", "The observed yield in SR^Z_1A is 3 events. The expected yield (post-fit SM events) is 5.7 ± 1.0."], "quote_supports": true, "support_why": "The cited sentences mention that observed and expected yields in the SRs are shown in Tables 10 and 11. The 'Total (post-fit) SM events' value of 5.7 ±1.0 and the observed yield of 3 events align with data from these tables, which are explicitly stated to cover the signal regions (SRs). Since SR^Z_1A is an SR, the paper provides the required yields.", "judged_by": "Qwen/QwQ-32B-AWQ"}
{"qid": "gf-11", "paper_id": "2001.06899", "quotes_by_model": {"?": ["experimental precision is improved with respect to previous LHC results because of the increased size of the data sample and advanced heavy-flavor tagging techniques.", "The algorithm used in the analysis, the combined secondary vertex (Version 2), is described in Ref. [ 42 ] .", "The analysis uses a 'medium' operating point, which corresponds to approximately 10 (60)% tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ ( $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ ) quark jets and a misidentification probability of 1% for a light jet.", "The analysis uses a 'medium' operating point, which corresponds to approximately 10 (60)% tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ ($\\mathup{{{b}}}$ ) quark jets and a misidentification probability of 1% for a light jet.", "SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of $\\Delta R<0.3$ around the jet axis.", "In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\\text{~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\\text{~jets}$ events. The ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{light~jets}$ mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range.", "The SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of ΔR<0.3 around the jet axis.", "In the jet p_{mathrm{T}} range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for Z+c jets and from 45.9 to 60.7% for Z+b jets events. The Z+light jets mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same p_{mathrm{T}} range.", "In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}~jets}$ events. The ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{light~jets}$ mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range.", "The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure. To reduce the combinatorics the best pair (triplet) of jets is chosen by minimizing the reconstructed and nominal mass of the $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson (top quark). From this optimization, the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet candidates from top quark and $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson decays are identified. The event is kept if these candidates pass the jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4 .", "In the resulting sample of the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jet candidates about half have correct flavor assignment whereas the other half constitute mostly $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets that are misidentified as $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets.", "The $M_{\text{SV}}$ template for $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets is derived from a high-purity data sample of $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ events decaying to final states of ${\\mathup{{{e}}}}{\\mathup{}}$ $+\\geq$ 2 jets with at least one b-tagged jet.", "Good agreement between the data-driven template shape and the simulation is observed.", "The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure...", "The HF tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets are estimated in simulation and corrected by the efficiency scale factors as described in Section 5.2 . The systematic uncertainties of the efficiency scale factors of $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets with $30<p_{\\mathrm{T}}<100\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ and $\\lvert\\eta\\rvert<2.4$ are ${\\approx}3.5\\%$ and ${\\approx}1.4\\%$ , respectively [ 42 ] ."], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["The discrimination of HF jets from light-flavor quark and gluon jets, referred to as light jets in the following, is achieved by constructing a discriminator variable from tracks and secondary vertex (SV) characteristics.", "Artificial neural network algorithms are used to combine specific properties of the HF quarks, long lifetime and substantial mass, to build the discriminator.", "The algorithm used in the analysis, the combined secondary vertex (Version 2), is described in Ref. [ 42 ] .", "The analysis uses a “medium” operating point, which corresponds to approximately 10 (60)% tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ ( $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ ) quark jets and a misidentification probability of 1% for a light jet.", "The efficiencies of tagging ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events, $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{c}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$ and $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{b}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$ , are calculated as the ratio between numbers of selected ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events, respectively, in the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ and the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ samples.", "In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events.", "The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure. To reduce the combinatorics the best pair (triplet) of jets is chosen by minimizing the reconstructed and nominal mass of the $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson (top quark). From this optimization, the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet candidates from top quark and $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson decays are identified. The event is kept if these candidates pass the jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4 .", "Heavy-flavor tagging efficiency: The HF tagging efficiencies for c and b jets are estimated in simulation and corrected by the efficiency scale factors as described in Section 5.2 . The systematic uncertainties of the efficiency scale factors of c jets and b jets with 30<pT<100 GeV and |η|<2.4 are ≈3.5% and ≈1.4% , respectively [ 42 ] .", "The HF tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets are estimated in simulation and corrected by the efficiency scale factors as described in Section 5.2 . The systematic uncertainties of the efficiency scale factors of $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets with $30<p_{\\mathrm{T}}<100\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ and $\\lvert\\eta\rvert<2.4$ are ${\\approx}3.5\\%$ and ${\\approx}1.4\\%$ , respectively [ 42 ] ."]}, "answers_by_model": {"?": ["The paper states that the b-tagging efficiency scale factors have a systematic uncertainty of ≈1.4% for b jets in the specified kinematic range, but does not name the algorithm or working point.", "The paper uses an 'adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm' for SV tagging. The tagging efficiencies are 8.3–11.3% for c jets and 45.9–60.7% for b jets, with a light-jet mistag rate of 0.3–1.0%. No specific b-tagging working point is mentioned.", "The paper uses an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm (referenced as [43]) for SV tagging. The tagging efficiencies for c jets range from 8.3% to 11.3%, and for b jets from 45.9% to 60.7% in the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range of 30-200 GeV. The mistagging rate for light jets increases from 0.3% to 1.0% in the same range. No specific b-tagging working point is mentioned.", "The paper uses an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm for tagging. The b-jet tagging efficiency ranges from 45.9% to 60.7%, while the light-jet mistag rate ranges from 0.3% to 1.0% in the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range of 30–200 GeV. The working point is not explicitly stated.", "The paper uses the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm with a 'medium' working point. It has 10% tagging efficiency for c-jets, 60% for b-jets, and a 1% misidentification probability for light jets.", "The paper uses the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm with a 'medium' working point. This provides 10% tagging efficiency for c-jets, 60% for b-jets, and a 1% misidentification probability for light jets.", "The paper uses the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm with the 'medium' working point. This provides 10% tagging efficiency for c-jets, 60% for b-jets, and a 1% misidentification probability for light jets.", "The text refers to an unspecified 'HF tagging requirement' for b/c jet identification. Validation shows ~50% of c-jet candidates are correctly identified, with the rest mostly misidentified b-jets. b-jets are selected using a top-antitop sample requiring at least one b-tagged jet. No explicit algorithm name or working point is provided."], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["The b-tagging algorithm used is the combined secondary vertex (Version 2). The medium operating point has tagging efficiencies of approximately 10% for c quark jets and 60% for b quark jets, with a misidentification probability of 1% for light jets.", "The efficiencies of tagging ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events, $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{c}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$ and $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{b}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$ , are calculated as the ratio between numbers of selected ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events, respectively, in the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ and the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ samples. In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events.", "The efficiency of tagging ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events, $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{c}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$ and $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{b}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$, are calculated as the ratio between numbers of selected ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events, respectively, in the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ and the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ samples. In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events.", "The heavy-flavor tagging efficiency scale factors for c jets and b jets have systematic uncertainties of approximately 3.5% and 1.4%, respectively, for jets with 30<pT<100 GeV and |η|<2.4.", "The paper uses the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm for b-tagging. It operates at a “medium” working point with approximately 10% tagging efficiency for c quark jets, 60% for b quark jets, and a 1% misidentification probability for light jets.", "The systematic uncertainties of the efficiency scale factors of $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets with $30<p_{\\mathrm{T}}<100\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ and $\\lvert\\eta\rvert<2.4$ are ${\\approx}3.5\\%$ and ${\\approx}1.4\\%$ , respectively.", "The systematic uncertainties of the efficiency scale factors of b jets with $30<p_{\\mathrm{T}}<100\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ and $\\lvert\\eta\rvert<2.4$ are ${\\approx}1.4\\%$ .", "The text mentions HF tagging but does not specify the algorithm or working point used.", "The text mentions the performance of the tagging efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events, which range from 8.3 to 11.3% and from 45.9 to 60.7%, respectively, in the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV.", "The text mentions the use of the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm for b-tagging. It operates at a “medium” working point with tagging efficiencies of approximately 10% for c quark jets and 60% for b quark jets, and a misidentification probability of 1% for light jets."]}, "verdicts": [], "all_quotes": ["experimental precision is improved with respect to previous LHC results because of the increased size of the data sample and advanced heavy-flavor tagging techniques.", "The algorithm used in the analysis, the combined secondary vertex (Version 2), is described in Ref. [ 42 ] .", "The analysis uses a 'medium' operating point, which corresponds to approximately 10 (60)% tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ ( $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ ) quark jets and a misidentification probability of 1% for a light jet.", "The analysis uses a 'medium' operating point, which corresponds to approximately 10 (60)% tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ ($\\mathup{{{b}}}$ ) quark jets and a misidentification probability of 1% for a light jet.", "SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of $\\Delta R<0.3$ around the jet axis.", "In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\\text{~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\\text{~jets}$ events. The ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\\text{light~jets}$ mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range.", "The SV is reconstructed using an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm [ 43 ] from selected tracks within a cone of ΔR<0.3 around the jet axis.", "In the jet p_{mathrm{T}} range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for Z+c jets and from 45.9 to 60.7% for Z+b jets events. The Z+light jets mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same p_{mathrm{T}} range.", "In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}~jets}$ events. The ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{light~jets}$ mistagging rate increases from 0.3 to 1.0% in the same $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range.", "The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure. To reduce the combinatorics the best pair (triplet) of jets is chosen by minimizing the reconstructed and nominal mass of the $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson (top quark). From this optimization, the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet candidates from top quark and $\\mathup{{{W}}}$ boson decays are identified. The event is kept if these candidates pass the jet HF tagging requirement described in Section 4 .", "In the resulting sample of the $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jet candidates about half have correct flavor assignment whereas the other half constitute mostly $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets that are misidentified as $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets.", "The $M_{\text{SV}}$ template for $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets is derived from a high-purity data sample of $\\mathup{{{t}}}$ $\\mathup{{\\overline{{{\\mathup{{{t}}}}}}}}$ events decaying to final states of ${\\mathup{{{e}}}}{\\mathup{}}$ $+\\geq$ 2 jets with at least one b-tagged jet.", "Good agreement between the data-driven template shape and the simulation is observed.", "The $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jet identification is performed with the following procedure...", "The HF tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets are estimated in simulation and corrected by the efficiency scale factors as described in Section 5.2 . The systematic uncertainties of the efficiency scale factors of $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets with $30<p_{\\mathrm{T}}<100\\,\\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ and $\\lvert\\eta\\rvert<2.4$ are ${\\approx}3.5\\%$ and ${\\approx}1.4\\%$ , respectively [ 42 ] .", "The discrimination of HF jets from light-flavor quark and gluon jets, referred to as light jets in the following, is achieved by constructing a discriminator variable from tracks and secondary vertex (SV) characteristics.", "Artificial neural network algorithms are used to combine specific properties of the HF quarks, long lifetime and substantial mass, to build the discriminator.", "The analysis uses a “medium” operating point, which corresponds to approximately 10 (60)% tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ ( $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ ) quark jets and a misidentification probability of 1% for a light jet.", "The efficiencies of tagging ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events, $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{c}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$ and $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{b}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$ , are calculated as the ratio between numbers of selected ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events, respectively, in the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ and the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ samples.", "In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events.", "Heavy-flavor tagging efficiency: The HF tagging efficiencies for c and b jets are estimated in simulation and corrected by the efficiency scale factors as described in Section 5.2 . The systematic uncertainties of the efficiency scale factors of c jets and b jets with 30<pT<100 GeV and |η|<2.4 are ≈3.5% and ≈1.4% , respectively [ 42 ] .", "The HF tagging efficiencies for $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets are estimated in simulation and corrected by the efficiency scale factors as described in Section 5.2 . The systematic uncertainties of the efficiency scale factors of $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets with $30<p_{\\mathrm{T}}<100\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ and $\\lvert\\eta\rvert<2.4$ are ${\\approx}3.5\\%$ and ${\\approx}1.4\\%$ , respectively [ 42 ] ."], "quote": "experimental precision is improved with respect to previous LHC results because of the increased size of the data sample and advanced heavy-flavor tagging techniques.", "answer": true, "answers": ["The b-tagging algorithm used is the combined secondary vertex (Version 2). The medium operating point has tagging efficiencies of approximately 10% for c quark jets and 60% for b quark jets, with a misidentification probability of 1% for light jets.", "The efficiencies of tagging ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events, $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{c}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$ and $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{b}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$ , are calculated as the ratio between numbers of selected ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events, respectively, in the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ and the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ samples. In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events.", "The efficiency of tagging ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ events, $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{c}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$ and $\\epsilon^{{\\mathup{{{b}}}}}_{\text{tag}}$, are calculated as the ratio between numbers of selected ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events, respectively, in the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{HF~jets}$ and the ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt\text{jets}$ samples. In the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV the efficiencies vary only slightly and range from 8.3 to 11.3% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and from 45.9 to 60.7% for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events.", "The heavy-flavor tagging efficiency scale factors for c jets and b jets have systematic uncertainties of approximately 3.5% and 1.4%, respectively, for jets with 30<pT<100 GeV and |η|<2.4.", "The paper states that the b-tagging efficiency scale factors have a systematic uncertainty of ≈1.4% for b jets in the specified kinematic range, but does not name the algorithm or working point.", "The paper uses an 'adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm' for SV tagging. The tagging efficiencies are 8.3–11.3% for c jets and 45.9–60.7% for b jets, with a light-jet mistag rate of 0.3–1.0%. No specific b-tagging working point is mentioned.", "The paper uses an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm (referenced as [43]) for SV tagging. The tagging efficiencies for c jets range from 8.3% to 11.3%, and for b jets from 45.9% to 60.7% in the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range of 30-200 GeV. The mistagging rate for light jets increases from 0.3% to 1.0% in the same range. No specific b-tagging working point is mentioned.", "The paper uses an adaptive vertex reconstruction algorithm for tagging. The b-jet tagging efficiency ranges from 45.9% to 60.7%, while the light-jet mistag rate ranges from 0.3% to 1.0% in the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range of 30–200 GeV. The working point is not explicitly stated.", "The paper uses the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm for b-tagging. It operates at a “medium” working point with approximately 10% tagging efficiency for c quark jets, 60% for b quark jets, and a 1% misidentification probability for light jets.", "The paper uses the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm with a 'medium' working point. It has 10% tagging efficiency for c-jets, 60% for b-jets, and a 1% misidentification probability for light jets.", "The paper uses the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm with a 'medium' working point. This provides 10% tagging efficiency for c-jets, 60% for b-jets, and a 1% misidentification probability for light jets.", "The paper uses the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm with the 'medium' working point. This provides 10% tagging efficiency for c-jets, 60% for b-jets, and a 1% misidentification probability for light jets.", "The systematic uncertainties of the efficiency scale factors of $\\mathup{{{c}}}$ jets and $\\mathup{{{b}}}$ jets with $30<p_{\\mathrm{T}}<100\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ and $\\lvert\\eta\rvert<2.4$ are ${\\approx}3.5\\%$ and ${\\approx}1.4\\%$ , respectively.", "The systematic uncertainties of the efficiency scale factors of b jets with $30<p_{\\mathrm{T}}<100\\,\text{Ge\\hskip-0.80002ptV}$ and $\\lvert\\eta\rvert<2.4$ are ${\\approx}1.4\\%$ .", "The text mentions HF tagging but does not specify the algorithm or working point used.", "The text mentions the performance of the tagging efficiencies for ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{c}}}}\text{~jets}$ and ${\\mathup{{{Z}}}}\\hskip-2.5pt+\\hskip-1.49994pt{\\mathup{{{b}}}}\text{~jets}$ events, which range from 8.3 to 11.3% and from 45.9 to 60.7%, respectively, in the jet $p_{\\mathrm{T}}$ range between 30 and 200  GeV.", "The text mentions the use of the combined secondary vertex (Version 2) algorithm for b-tagging. It operates at a “medium” working point with tagging efficiencies of approximately 10% for c quark jets and 60% for b quark jets, and a misidentification probability of 1% for light jets.", "The text refers to an unspecified 'HF tagging requirement' for b/c jet identification. Validation shows ~50% of c-jet candidates are correctly identified, with the rest mostly misidentified b-jets. b-jets are selected using a top-antitop sample requiring at least one b-tagged jet. No explicit algorithm name or working point is provided."], "quote_supports": true, "support_why": "The sentences explicitly state the algorithm (combined secondary vertex Version 2), the 'medium' working point, and its performance (10% c-tag efficiency, 60% b-tag efficiency, 1% light jet misidentification). These directly answer the question.", "judged_by": "Qwen/QwQ-32B-AWQ"}
