exclusion limits on $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ masses are up to 300 GeV better for models featuring only $\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}\\to Z\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ decays compared with models only considering the $\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}\\to h\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ decays.", "Masses of the $\tilde{t}_{2}$ up to 875 GeV are excluded at 95% CL for a $\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass of about 350 GeV   and $\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ masses of approximately 520 (450) GeV are excluded for $\tilde{t}_{2}$ masses of 650 (800) GeV .", "The limits exclude, at 95% confidence level, $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ masses up to 875 GeV in models featuring $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ production and $\\tilde{t}_{2}\\to Z\\tilde{t}_{1}$ with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}}}$ decays.", "The observed 95% CL lower limit on the $\\tilde{t}_2$ mass is 875 GeV for a $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$ mass of about 350 GeV.", "The observed 95% CL lower limit on the $\\tilde{t}_2$ mass is 875 GeV.", "The observed 95% CL lower limit on the t2 mass is 875 GeV.", "The observed 95% CL lower limit on the t₂ (t̃₁) mass is 1220 GeV when the χ̃² mass is 925 GeV.", "The text mentions that $\tilde{t}_{2}$ masses up to 875 GeV are excluded at 95% confidence level."], "quote_supports": true, "support_why": "The sentences explicitly state that 'Masses of the 𝑡̃₂ up to 875 GeV are excluded at 95% CL' and similar phrasing, directly providing the 95% CL lower limit on the 𝑡̃₂ mass as 875 GeV. This matches the question's requirement.", "judged_by": "Qwen/QwQ-32B-AWQ"}
{"qid": "gf-14", "paper_id": "2006.05880", "quotes_by_model": {"?": ["Searches for top squark production in events involving Higgs or $Z$ bosons have been performed previously by both ATLAS [ 20 , 21 ] and CMS [ 22 , 23 ] .", "For each signal hypothesis, the background fit is repeated taking into account the signal contamination in the CRs, which is found to be below 12% for signal models close to the existing exclusion limits [ 20 ] .", "For signal models close to the existing exclusion limits [ 20 ] .", "These results improve upon the existing limits on the $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass in this model by approximately 300 GeV [ 20 ].", "Results are compared with the observed limits obtained by the previous ATLAS search in Ref. [ 20 ] .", "extending beyond the previous limits on the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass from Ref. [ 89 ] by up to 160 GeV.", "Compared with previous limits, these results extend the mass parameter space exclusion by up to 300 GeV in $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass and by up to 160 GeV in $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass in the considered $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ model.", "The limits exclude, at 95% confidence level, $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ masses up to 1220 GeV in models featuring $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ production and $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to Z/h\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ decays, and $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ masses up to 875 GeV in models featuring $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ production and $\\tilde{t}_{2}\\to Z\\tilde{t}_{1}$ with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise .64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ decays."], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["Searches for top squarks in final states with Higgs or Z bosons have been performed by ATLAS [2012] and CMS [2011].", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive signal regions defined in Section 3.2. The results are interpreted as exclusion limits on the mass of the gluino and the lightest neutralino in the context of simplified models of supersymmetry (SUSY).", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of the signal regions.", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}$ .", "These results improve upon the existing limits on the $\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass in this model by approximately 300 GeV [20]", "The results are compared to the observed limits obtained by the previous ATLAS search in Ref. [20]", "These results improve upon the existing limits on the $\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass in this model by approximately 300 GeV [ 20 ] .", "Masses of the $\tilde{t}_{2}$ up to 875 GeV are excluded at 95% CL for a $\tilde{\\chi}_{1}^{0}$ mass of about 350 GeV and $\tilde{\\chi}_{1}^{0}$ masses of approximately 520 GeV are excluded for $\tilde{t}_{2}$ masses of 650 GeV and 800 GeV, extending the previous limits on the stop mass by up to 160 GeV.", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the 139 fb−1 data from the 2015-2018 data-taking periods", "The results are obtained from the statistical combination of the 139 fb−1 data from the 2015-2018 data-taking periods", "The results are obtained from the statistical combination of the 2015-2018 data-taking periods", "Compared with previous limits, these results extend the mass parameter space exclusion by up to 300 GeV in $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass and by up to 160 GeV in $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass in the considered $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ model.", "Compared with previous ATLAS results, these results extend the mass parameter space exclusion by up to 300 GeV in $\tilde{t}$ mass and up to 100 GeV in $\tilde{\\chi}_{1}$ mass.", "These results extend the mass parameter space exclusion by up to 300 GeV in $\tilde{t}$ mass and up to 100 GeV in $\tilde{\\chi}_{1}$ mass."]}, "answers_by_model": {"?": ["The 2006.05880 analysis extends previous ATLAS limits by up to 300 GeV in $\\tilde{t}_1$ mass and up to 160 GeV in $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$ mass in the $\\tilde{t}_2$ model.", "The 2006.05880 analysis's stop-mass limits improve upon the previous ATLAS result by approximately 300 GeV, as stated in the text. The comparison is explicitly made with Ref. [20], which is cited as the prior ATLAS search.", "The new analysis extends the previous ATLAS limits (Ref. [89]) on the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass by up to 160 GeV.", "The new analysis extends the previous ATLAS limits (Ref. [89]) on the $\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass by up to 160 GeV.", "The new results extend previous ATLAS limits by excluding up to 300 GeV more in $\\tilde{t}_1$ mass and 160 GeV more in $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$ mass in specific models.", "The new results extend the previous limits on the $\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass from Ref. [89] by up to 160 GeV.", "The paper acknowledges prior ATLAS searches (references [20,21]) but does not explicitly state how the current stop-mass limits compare to them.", "The stop-mass limits in this analysis improve upon the previous ATLAS result (Ref. [20]) by approximately 300 GeV for certain scenarios.", "The stop-mass limits in this analysis improve upon the previous ATLAS result by approximately 300 GeV. The comparison is explicitly stated in the text and figures.", "The text acknowledges prior ATLAS searches but does not state how the current analysis's stop-mass limits compare to them.", "The text references existing exclusion limits [20] but does not explicitly compare the current stop-mass limits to the previous ATLAS result."], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["The results are compared to the observed limits obtained by the previous ATLAS search in Ref. [20]", "The results are obtained from the statistical combination of the 2015-2018 data-taking periods", "The stop-mass limits in 2006.05880 improve upon the previous ATLAS result by approximately 300 GeV.", "The text mentions that searches for top squarks in final states with Higgs or Z bosons have been performed by ATLAS and CMS. The text also mentions that the current analysis is performed with 2006.05880. ", "The text mentions that the results are compared to the previous ATLAS results and other experiments for the same model, but it does not provide specific details on how the stop-mass limits compare.", "The text mentions that the results extend the mass parameter space exclusion by up to 300 GeV in $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass and by up to 160 GeV in $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass in the considered $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ model.", "The text mentions the mass limits for $\tilde{t}$ and $\tilde{\\chi}_{1}$ and compares these to previous results. The text does not give the previous ATLAS result.", "The text states that the stop mass limits from this analysis are compared to the previous ATLAS result, and that the new analysis extends the previous limits by up to 160 GeV.", "These results are compared to the previous ATLAS results in Ref. [20]."]}, "verdicts": [], "all_quotes": ["Searches for top squark production in events involving Higgs or $Z$ bosons have been performed previously by both ATLAS [ 20 , 21 ] and CMS [ 22 , 23 ] .", "For each signal hypothesis, the background fit is repeated taking into account the signal contamination in the CRs, which is found to be below 12% for signal models close to the existing exclusion limits [ 20 ] .", "For signal models close to the existing exclusion limits [ 20 ] .", "These results improve upon the existing limits on the $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass in this model by approximately 300 GeV [ 20 ].", "Results are compared with the observed limits obtained by the previous ATLAS search in Ref. [ 20 ] .", "extending beyond the previous limits on the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass from Ref. [ 89 ] by up to 160 GeV.", "Compared with previous limits, these results extend the mass parameter space exclusion by up to 300 GeV in $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass and by up to 160 GeV in $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass in the considered $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ model.", "The limits exclude, at 95% confidence level, $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ masses up to 1220 GeV in models featuring $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ production and $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to Z/h\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ decays, and $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ masses up to 875 GeV in models featuring $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ production and $\\tilde{t}_{2}\\to Z\\tilde{t}_{1}$ with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise .64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ decays.", "Searches for top squarks in final states with Higgs or Z bosons have been performed by ATLAS [2012] and CMS [2011].", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive signal regions defined in Section 3.2. The results are interpreted as exclusion limits on the mass of the gluino and the lightest neutralino in the context of simplified models of supersymmetry (SUSY).", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of the signal regions.", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}$ .", "These results improve upon the existing limits on the $\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass in this model by approximately 300 GeV [20]", "The results are compared to the observed limits obtained by the previous ATLAS search in Ref. [20]", "These results improve upon the existing limits on the $\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass in this model by approximately 300 GeV [ 20 ] .", "Masses of the $\tilde{t}_{2}$ up to 875 GeV are excluded at 95% CL for a $\tilde{\\chi}_{1}^{0}$ mass of about 350 GeV and $\tilde{\\chi}_{1}^{0}$ masses of approximately 520 GeV are excluded for $\tilde{t}_{2}$ masses of 650 GeV and 800 GeV, extending the previous limits on the stop mass by up to 160 GeV.", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the 139 fb−1 data from the 2015-2018 data-taking periods", "The results are obtained from the statistical combination of the 139 fb−1 data from the 2015-2018 data-taking periods", "The results are obtained from the statistical combination of the 2015-2018 data-taking periods", "Compared with previous ATLAS results, these results extend the mass parameter space exclusion by up to 300 GeV in $\tilde{t}$ mass and up to 100 GeV in $\tilde{\\chi}_{1}$ mass.", "These results extend the mass parameter space exclusion by up to 300 GeV in $\tilde{t}$ mass and up to 100 GeV in $\tilde{\\chi}_{1}$ mass."], "quote": "Searches for top squark production in events involving Higgs or $Z$ bosons have been performed previously by both ATLAS [ 20 , 21 ] and CMS [ 22 , 23 ] .", "answer": true, "answers": ["The 2006.05880 analysis extends previous ATLAS limits by up to 300 GeV in $\\tilde{t}_1$ mass and up to 160 GeV in $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$ mass in the $\\tilde{t}_2$ model.", "The 2006.05880 analysis's stop-mass limits improve upon the previous ATLAS result by approximately 300 GeV, as stated in the text. The comparison is explicitly made with Ref. [20], which is cited as the prior ATLAS search.", "The new analysis extends the previous ATLAS limits (Ref. [89]) on the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass by up to 160 GeV.", "The new analysis extends the previous ATLAS limits (Ref. [89]) on the $\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass by up to 160 GeV.", "The new results extend previous ATLAS limits by excluding up to 300 GeV more in $\\tilde{t}_1$ mass and 160 GeV more in $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$ mass in specific models.", "The new results extend the previous limits on the $\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass from Ref. [89] by up to 160 GeV.", "The paper acknowledges prior ATLAS searches (references [20,21]) but does not explicitly state how the current stop-mass limits compare to them.", "The results are compared to the observed limits obtained by the previous ATLAS search in Ref. [20]", "The results are obtained from the statistical combination of the 2015-2018 data-taking periods", "The stop-mass limits in 2006.05880 improve upon the previous ATLAS result by approximately 300 GeV.", "The stop-mass limits in this analysis improve upon the previous ATLAS result (Ref. [20]) by approximately 300 GeV for certain scenarios.", "The stop-mass limits in this analysis improve upon the previous ATLAS result by approximately 300 GeV. The comparison is explicitly stated in the text and figures.", "The text acknowledges prior ATLAS searches but does not state how the current analysis's stop-mass limits compare to them.", "The text mentions that searches for top squarks in final states with Higgs or Z bosons have been performed by ATLAS and CMS. The text also mentions that the current analysis is performed with 2006.05880. ", "The text mentions that the results are compared to the previous ATLAS results and other experiments for the same model, but it does not provide specific details on how the stop-mass limits compare.", "The text mentions that the results extend the mass parameter space exclusion by up to 300 GeV in $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ mass and by up to 160 GeV in $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ mass in the considered $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ model.", "The text mentions the mass limits for $\tilde{t}$ and $\tilde{\\chi}_{1}$ and compares these to previous results. The text does not give the previous ATLAS result.", "The text references existing exclusion limits [20] but does not explicitly compare the current stop-mass limits to the previous ATLAS result.", "The text states that the stop mass limits from this analysis are compared to the previous ATLAS result, and that the new analysis extends the previous limits by up to 160 GeV.", "These results are compared to the previous ATLAS results in Ref. [20]."], "quote_supports": true, "support_why": "The sentences explicitly state that the paper's results improve upon and extend the previous ATLAS limits (Ref. [20]) by specific amounts (300 GeV for $\\tilde{t}_1$ and 160 GeV for $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$), directly addressing the comparison requested.", "judged_by": "Qwen/QwQ-32B-AWQ"}
{"qid": "gf-06", "paper_id": "2006.05880", "quotes_by_model": {"?": ["Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production ( $t\\bar{t}$ ) background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ ( $\\ell\\equiv e,\\mu$ ) decay or a pair of $b$ -tagged jets originating from the $h\\to b\\bar{b}$ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production (t t̄) background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the Z→ℓ+ℓ− (ℓ≡e,μ) decay or a pair of b-tagged jets originating from the h→b b̄ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 $\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving $Z$ bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 $\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).", "The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).", "The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol ℓ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the Z boson mass (|m_{ℓℓ}−m_Z|<15 GeV, with m_Z=91.2 GeV) is required.", "Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction.", "Signal region SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ is optimised for large $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}-\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_1\\to t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to h/Z\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model.", "Models with small mass differences between the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ are targeted with SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair ( $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ ).", "Two SRs are optimised for the $\\tilde{t}_2\\to Z\\tilde{t}_1$ with $\\tilde{t}_1\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ , targeting small and large mass splittings between the $\\tilde{t}_2$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ , respectively.", "SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ features upper bounds on the $p_{\\text{T}}$ of the third leading lepton and on $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ , as well as no requirement on the number of $b$ -tagged jets since they are likely to be soft in this scenario.", "SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ also includes an upper bound on the third leading lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ but requires the presence of $b$ -tagged jets and large $E_{\\text{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ and $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ .", "Models with small mass differences between the [...] are targeted with SR^Z_{1B}, which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair (p_{T}^{ℓℓ}).", "Two SRs are optimised for the [...] model, SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B}, targeting small and large mass splittings between the [...] respectively.", "Due to the overall soft kinematics [...] SR^Z_{2A} [...] features upper bounds [...] SR^Z_{2B} [...] requires the presence of b-tagged jets and large E_T^{miss} and p_{T}^{ℓℓ}.", "The requirement on the number of signal leptons [...] makes the SR^Z_{1A} and SR^Z_{1B} selections insensitive to potential contributions [...] The acceptance [...] was found to depend linearly [...] The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution [...] to SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B}.", "The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol $\\ell$ ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the $Z$ boson mass ( $|m_{\\ell\\ell}-m_{Z}|<15$ GeV , with $m_{Z}=91.2$ GeV ) is required.", "Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction .", "Signal region SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ is optimised for large $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}-\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to h/Z\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model.", "Two SRs are optimised for the $\\tilde{t}_{2}\\to Z\\tilde{t}_{1}$ with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ , targeting small and large mass splittings between the $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ , respectively.", "The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes the SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays...", "The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The dominant SM background contribution to the $3\\ell$ SRs is expected to originate from $t\\bar{t}Z$ production, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\bar{t}$ events).", "The normalisation of the main backgrounds ( $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson in the $3\\ell$ selection; $t\\bar{t}$ in the $1\\ell$ selection) is obtained by fitting the yield from MC simulation to the observed data in dedicated control regions (CRs) enhanced in a particular background component, and then extrapolating this yield to the SRs.", "Analogously to the 3 $\\ell$ selection, the acceptance for mixed decay scenarios with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\tilde{t}_{1}\\rightarrow t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ has been found to depend linearly on the branching fraction of $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ to $t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ .", "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The estimates for the FNP background are validated in dedicated regions with selection criteria similar to those defining the 3 $\\ell$ SRs but with reduced contributions from processes with three prompt leptons.", "Requirement / Region\nVR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$\n\nVR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$", "The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6 .", "Requirement / Region\nCR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$\n\nVR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$\n\nCR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$\n\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$\n\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$", "To validate the background estimates and provide a statistically independent cross-check of the extrapolation to the SRs, three validation regions are defined, as shown in Table 6 . The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.", "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4, with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "To enhance fakes and be mutually exclusive with the SR, events in these VRs are required to have no SF-OS dilepton pairs and to have at least one different-flavour opposite-sign (DF-OS) dilepton pair.", "Number of signal leptons ≥ 3 (from Table 6 for CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, CR^Z_{VV}, VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}, and VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}})", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 $\\ell$ and 1 $\\ell$ SRs, respectively.", "To enhance the exclusion power in the SUSY models considered, a ‘shape-fit’ approach is used where several mutually exclusive bins in different kinematic variables are defined in order to take advantage of the different signal-to-background ratios in the different bins.", "The likelihood is built from the product of probability density functions describing the observed numbers of events in the SR and the associated CRs.", "Table 10 also shows upper limits (at the 95% CL) on the number of BSM events $S^{95}$ , and on the visible BSM cross-section $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{vis}}$", "The statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 .", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 ℓ and 1 ℓ SRs, respectively.", "Table 10: Observed and expected numbers of events in the 3 ℓ signal regions...", "Table 12: Selection criteria used in the shape-fit to derive the model-dependent exclusion limits...", "The shape-fit approach is used where several mutually exclusive bins in different kinematic variables are defined in order to take advantage of the different signal-to-background ratios in the different bins.", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 .", "A search for direct top squark pair production in events with a leptonically decaying $Z$ boson or a SM Higgs boson decaying into a $b$ -quark pair is presented."], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production ( $t\bar{t}$ ) background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ ( $\\ell\\equiv e,\\mu$ ) decay or a pair of $b$ -tagged jets originating from the $h\to b\bar{b}$ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "The mass difference between the $\tilde{t}_{1}$ and $\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ is set to be smaller than the $W$ boson mass, and the four-body decay $\tilde{t}_{1}\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ is assumed to occur, where $f$ and $f^{\\prime}$ are two fermions from the $W^{*}$ decay, such as $\tilde{t}_{1}\to b\\ell\nu\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ .", "The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).", "The selection requirements for each of these categories are described below.", "The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol $\\ell$ ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the $Z$ boson mass ( $|m_{\\ell\\ell}-m_{Z}|<15$ GeV , with $m_{Z}=91.2$ GeV ) is required.", "The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes the SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays with each $\tilde{t}_{1}\rightarrow t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ .", "The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6 .", "The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,}b\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.", "To enhance fakes and be mutually exclusive with the SR, events in these VRs are required to have no SF-OS dilepton pairs and to have at least one different-flavour opposite-sign (DF-OS) dilepton pair.", "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4, with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6.", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 $\\ell$ and 1 $\\ell$ SRs, respectively.", "Table 10 also shows upper limits (at the 95% CL) on the number of BSM events $S^{95}$ , and on the visible BSM cross-section $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{vis}}=S_{\textrm{obs}}^{95}/\\int\\!{\\mathcal{}L}\textrm{d}t$ , defined as the product of the production cross-section, acceptance and efficiency.", "The observed number of events and expected yields in all these bins are shown in Figures 8 and 9 .", "The last bin in each figure contains the overflow.", "The ‘FNP’ category represents the background from fake or non-prompt leptons.", "The expected distributions for selected signal models are also shown as dashed lines.", "The observed number of events and expected yields in all the 3 $\\ell$ SRs and bins used for the model-dependent exclusion limits.", "The bins of SR ${}_{1{\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ are separately combined with the bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ is used in the exclusion fits with no changes.", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 .", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ shown in Table 12 ."]}, "answers_by_model": {"?": ["All four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in a specific model using m^{3ℓ}_{T2}; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences using p_T^{ℓℓ}; SR^Z_{2A} targets small mass splittings in another model with soft kinematics (upper pT bounds); SR^Z_{2B} targets large mass splittings in that model with b-tagged jets and higher missing ET/p_T^{ℓℓ}.", "All four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_1$ model; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences between $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_2$ and $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$; SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B} target small and large mass splittings between $\\tilde{t}_2$ and $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$, respectively. Each uses specific kinematic requirements (e.g., $m^{3\\ell}_{\\text{T}2}$, $p_{\\text{T}}$ bounds, $b$-tags) to optimize sensitivity for their respective models.", "Regions requiring ≥3 leptons and their roles: \n- VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F}: Validate FNP lepton background estimates by probing lepton pT regimes and soft lepton requirements, while excluding SF-OS dilepton pairs. \n- CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}: Estimates t\\bar{t}Z background with specific MET and jet requirements. \n- CR^Z_{VV}: Estimates multi-boson background with MET and b-tagging constraints. \n- VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}: Validates t\\bar{t}Z background estimates. \n- VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}} and VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}: Validate multi-boson background estimates, focusing on jet multiplicity and b-tagging.", "The 3ℓ (three-lepton) region requires at least three leptons, and its role is to target top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The 3ℓ SRs (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles include setting exclusion limits for BSM scenarios via upper limits on BSM events and cross-sections (via Tables 10 and 12), and enhancing SUSY model exclusion power through shape-fit analyses of kinematic distributions (as in Figures 6 and 9).", "The 3ℓ SRs (three-lepton signal regions) require at least three leptons. Their role is related to background estimation, specifically targeting backgrounds like $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson processes.", "The 3ℓ SRs (three-lepton signal regions) require at least three leptons. Their role is to estimate Standard Model backgrounds (e.g., $t\\bar{t}Z$, multi-boson, fake/non-prompt leptons) by using control regions and Monte Carlo simulations for normalization and validation.", "The 3ℓ SRs require at least three leptons. Their role is implied to be signal regions where backgrounds (e.g., ttZ, multi-boson, fake/non-prompt leptons) are estimated, but the text does not explicitly describe their specific roles beyond their existence in the analysis.", "The 3ℓ region requires at least three leptons and is aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The 3ℓ region requires at least three leptons, and its role is to target top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The region requiring at least three leptons uses an SF-OS lepton pair from $Z$ decay and an additional lepton from top quark decay.", "The region requiring three leptons is when an SF-OS lepton pair (from Z decay) and an additional lepton (from top quark decay) are present. The SF-OS pair identifies the Z boson decay, while the additional lepton helps distinguish the signal from background.", "The regions requiring at least three leptons are SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, and SR^Z_{2B}. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in the first model using m^{3ℓ}_{T2}; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences using p_T^{ℓℓ}; SR^Z_{2A} targets small mass splittings in the second model with upper pT bounds and no b-tag requirements; SR^Z_{2B} targets large mass splittings in the second model with b-tagged jets and high E_T^{miss}/p_T^{ℓℓ}.", "The regions requiring at least three leptons are the 3ℓ SRs (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}). Their roles involve contributing to exclusion limits for BSM/SUSY models via kinematic bins (e.g., E_T^miss, p_T^ℓℓ) to differentiate signal/background ratios, as detailed in Tables 10/12 and figures 6/8.", "The regions requiring at least three leptons are: (1) VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} (validating FNP lepton background estimates with criteria similar to signal regions but reduced prompt lepton contributions), (2) CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z} (estimating t\\bar{t}Z background), (3) CR^Z_{VV} (estimating multi-boson background), and (4) VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, and VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag} (validating t\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson background estimates via jet multiplicity/extrapolation checks).", "The regions requiring three leptons include those with an SF-OS lepton pair from Z decay and an additional lepton from top quark decay, or a b-tagged jet pair from Higgs decay plus an additional lepton. The SF-OS pair identifies the Z boson, the b-tagged jets identify the Higgs boson, and the additional lepton comes from top quark decay.", "The regions requiring ≥3 leptons are: (1) VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} (for validating fake/non-prompt lepton backgrounds), (2) CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z} and CR^Z_{VV} (for estimating t\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson backgrounds), and (3) VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, and VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag} (for validating those estimates)."], "mistralai/Mistral-Small-24B-Instruct-2501": ["The analysis distinguishes signals from the SM top quark pair production background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ decay or a pair of $b$ -tagged jets originating from the $h\to b\bar{b}$ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "The presence of at least three signal leptons is required. The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes the SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays with each $\tilde{t}_{1}\rightarrow t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ . The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The region that requires at least three leptons is referred to as the 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The regions SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ require at least three signal leptons. The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes these selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays with each $\tilde{t}_{1}\rightarrow t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ . The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The regions VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ and VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ require at least three leptons and are used for probing the lepton $p_{\text{T}}$ regime and including soft lepton requirements, respectively. The control regions CR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ and CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ are used for the $t\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection. The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ region validates the $t\bar{t}Z$ background estimate, while the VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,}b\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, focusing on jet multiplicity and releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto, respectively.", "The regions VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ and VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ require at least three leptons. VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ probes the lepton $p_{\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$, while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$. The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,}b\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.", "The regions VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ and VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ require at least three leptons. VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ probes the lepton $p_{\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$, while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$. The control regions CR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ and CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ also require at least three leptons for the background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection.", "The regions that require at least three leptons are the 3 $\\ell$ SRs, which are used for model-dependent exclusion limits. The specific regions mentioned are SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$, SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$, and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$. The role of these regions is to provide observed and expected yields for the exclusion limits.", "The text describes two exclusive categories of events: one requiring at least three leptons (3 ℓ selection) aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons, and another requiring exactly one lepton (1 ℓ selection) aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons.", "The text mentions several regions (SRs) and their roles in the analysis, but it does not specify which regions require at least three leptons. The regions mentioned are SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ , and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The text mentions several signal regions (SRs) that require at least three leptons, including SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$, SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$, and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$.", "The text mentions specific signal regions (SRs) that require at least three leptons, including SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$, and provides details about their roles in the analysis. These regions are used in exclusion fits and their observed and expected yields are shown in figures and tables.", "The text mentions the requirement of a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ decay and the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event. It also mentions the four-body decay $\tilde{t}_{1}\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ where $f$ and $f^{\\prime}$ are two fermions from the $W^{*}$ decay, such as $\tilde{t}_{1}\to b\\ell\nu\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ ."]}, "verdicts": [], "all_quotes": ["Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production ( $t\\bar{t}$ ) background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ ( $\\ell\\equiv e,\\mu$ ) decay or a pair of $b$ -tagged jets originating from the $h\\to b\\bar{b}$ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production (t t̄) background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the Z→ℓ+ℓ− (ℓ≡e,μ) decay or a pair of b-tagged jets originating from the h→b b̄ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 $\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving $Z$ bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 $\\ell$ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).", "The events are classified into two exclusive categories: at least three leptons (referred to as 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons), or exactly one lepton (referred to as 1 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons).", "The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol ℓ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the Z boson mass (|m_{ℓℓ}−m_Z|<15 GeV, with m_Z=91.2 GeV) is required.", "Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction.", "Signal region SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ is optimised for large $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}-\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_1\\to t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to h/Z\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model.", "Models with small mass differences between the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ are targeted with SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair ( $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ ).", "Two SRs are optimised for the $\\tilde{t}_2\\to Z\\tilde{t}_1$ with $\\tilde{t}_1\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ , targeting small and large mass splittings between the $\\tilde{t}_2$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ , respectively.", "SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ features upper bounds on the $p_{\\text{T}}$ of the third leading lepton and on $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ , as well as no requirement on the number of $b$ -tagged jets since they are likely to be soft in this scenario.", "SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ also includes an upper bound on the third leading lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ but requires the presence of $b$ -tagged jets and large $E_{\\text{T}}^{\\text{miss}}$ and $p_{\\textrm{T}}^{\\ell\\ell}$ .", "Models with small mass differences between the [...] are targeted with SR^Z_{1B}, which instead imposes requirements on the transverse momentum of the SF-OS lepton pair (p_{T}^{ℓℓ}).", "Two SRs are optimised for the [...] model, SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B}, targeting small and large mass splittings between the [...] respectively.", "Due to the overall soft kinematics [...] SR^Z_{2A} [...] features upper bounds [...] SR^Z_{2B} [...] requires the presence of b-tagged jets and large E_T^{miss} and p_{T}^{ℓℓ}.", "The requirement on the number of signal leptons [...] makes the SR^Z_{1A} and SR^Z_{1B} selections insensitive to potential contributions [...] The acceptance [...] was found to depend linearly [...] The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution [...] to SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B}.", "The presence of at least three signal leptons (electrons or muons, referred to collectively with the symbol $\\ell$ ), with at least one SF-OS lepton pair whose invariant mass is compatible with the $Z$ boson mass ( $|m_{\\ell\\ell}-m_{Z}|<15$ GeV , with $m_{Z}=91.2$ GeV ) is required.", "Four overlapping signal regions (SRs) are optimised for the best discovery sensitivity, two for each of the simplified models described in Section Introduction .", "Signal region SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ is optimised for large $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}-\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ with $\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}\\to h/Z\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model.", "Two SRs are optimised for the $\\tilde{t}_{2}\\to Z\\tilde{t}_{1}$ with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ model, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ , targeting small and large mass splittings between the $\\tilde{t}_{2}$ and the $\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ , respectively.", "The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes the SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays...", "The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The dominant SM background contribution to the $3\\ell$ SRs is expected to originate from $t\\bar{t}Z$ production, with minor contributions from multi-boson production (mainly $WZ$ ) and backgrounds containing hadrons misidentified as leptons (hereafter referred to as ‘fake’ leptons) or non-prompt leptons from decays of hadrons (mainly in $t\\bar{t}$ events).", "The normalisation of the main backgrounds ( $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson in the $3\\ell$ selection; $t\\bar{t}$ in the $1\\ell$ selection) is obtained by fitting the yield from MC simulation to the observed data in dedicated control regions (CRs) enhanced in a particular background component, and then extrapolating this yield to the SRs.", "Analogously to the 3 $\\ell$ selection, the acceptance for mixed decay scenarios with $\\tilde{t}_{1}\\tilde{t}_{1}\\rightarrow t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ has been found to depend linearly on the branching fraction of $\\tilde{t}_{1}$ to $t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\textstyle\\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\textstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptstyle\\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{2}$}}$ .", "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The estimates for the FNP background are validated in dedicated regions with selection criteria similar to those defining the 3 $\\ell$ SRs but with reduced contributions from processes with three prompt leptons.", "Requirement / Region\nVR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$\n\nVR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$", "The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6 .", "Requirement / Region\nCR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$\n\nVR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$\n\nCR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$\n\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$\n\nVR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$", "To validate the background estimates and provide a statistically independent cross-check of the extrapolation to the SRs, three validation regions are defined, as shown in Table 6 . The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.", "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4, with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\\textrm{B}}}$ .", "To enhance fakes and be mutually exclusive with the SR, events in these VRs are required to have no SF-OS dilepton pairs and to have at least one different-flavour opposite-sign (DF-OS) dilepton pair.", "Number of signal leptons ≥ 3 (from Table 6 for CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, CR^Z_{VV}, VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}}, and VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}})", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 $\\ell$ and 1 $\\ell$ SRs, respectively.", "To enhance the exclusion power in the SUSY models considered, a ‘shape-fit’ approach is used where several mutually exclusive bins in different kinematic variables are defined in order to take advantage of the different signal-to-background ratios in the different bins.", "The likelihood is built from the product of probability density functions describing the observed numbers of events in the SR and the associated CRs.", "Table 10 also shows upper limits (at the 95% CL) on the number of BSM events $S^{95}$ , and on the visible BSM cross-section $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{vis}}$", "The statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 .", "The observed number of events and expected yields are shown in Tables 10 and 11 for each of the inclusive 3 ℓ and 1 ℓ SRs, respectively.", "Table 10: Observed and expected numbers of events in the 3 ℓ signal regions...", "Table 12: Selection criteria used in the shape-fit to derive the model-dependent exclusion limits...", "The shape-fit approach is used where several mutually exclusive bins in different kinematic variables are defined in order to take advantage of the different signal-to-background ratios in the different bins.", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 .", "A search for direct top squark pair production in events with a leptonically decaying $Z$ boson or a SM Higgs boson decaying into a $b$ -quark pair is presented.", "Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production ( $t\bar{t}$ ) background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ ( $\\ell\\equiv e,\\mu$ ) decay or a pair of $b$ -tagged jets originating from the $h\to b\bar{b}$ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "The mass difference between the $\tilde{t}_{1}$ and $\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$ is set to be smaller than the $W$ boson mass, and the four-body decay $\tilde{t}_{1}\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ is assumed to occur, where $f$ and $f^{\\prime}$ are two fermions from the $W^{*}$ decay, such as $\tilde{t}_{1}\to b\\ell\nu\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ .", "The selection requirements for each of these categories are described below.", "The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes the SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays with each $\tilde{t}_{1}\rightarrow t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ .", "The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4 , with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6 .", "The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,}b\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.", "Two VRs are defined as detailed in Table 4, with VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ probing the lepton $p_{\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ , while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The two dedicated control regions used for the $t\bar{t}Z$ (CR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ ) and multi-boson (CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ ) background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection are defined in Table 6.", "Table 10 also shows upper limits (at the 95% CL) on the number of BSM events $S^{95}$ , and on the visible BSM cross-section $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{vis}}=S_{\textrm{obs}}^{95}/\\int\\!{\\mathcal{}L}\textrm{d}t$ , defined as the product of the production cross-section, acceptance and efficiency.", "The observed number of events and expected yields in all these bins are shown in Figures 8 and 9 .", "The last bin in each figure contains the overflow.", "The ‘FNP’ category represents the background from fake or non-prompt leptons.", "The expected distributions for selected signal models are also shown as dashed lines.", "The observed number of events and expected yields in all the 3 $\\ell$ SRs and bins used for the model-dependent exclusion limits.", "The bins of SR ${}_{1{\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ are separately combined with the bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ is used in the exclusion fits with no changes.", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ and SR ${}_{1{\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ shown in Table 12 .", "These results are obtained from the statistical combination of SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and the shape-fit bins of SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ shown in Table 12 ."], "quote": "Unlike other top squark models, these signals can be efficiently distinguished from the SM top quark pair production ( $t\\bar{t}$ ) background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ ( $\\ell\\equiv e,\\mu$ ) decay or a pair of $b$ -tagged jets originating from the $h\\to b\\bar{b}$ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "answer": true, "answers": ["All four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in a specific model using m^{3ℓ}_{T2}; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences using p_T^{ℓℓ}; SR^Z_{2A} targets small mass splittings in another model with soft kinematics (upper pT bounds); SR^Z_{2B} targets large mass splittings in that model with b-tagged jets and higher missing ET/p_T^{ℓℓ}.", "All four signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in the $\\tilde{t}_1$ model; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences between $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_2$ and $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$; SR^Z_{2A} and SR^Z_{2B} target small and large mass splittings between $\\tilde{t}_2$ and $\\tilde{\\chi}^0_1$, respectively. Each uses specific kinematic requirements (e.g., $m^{3\\ell}_{\\text{T}2}$, $p_{\\text{T}}$ bounds, $b$-tags) to optimize sensitivity for their respective models.", "Regions requiring ≥3 leptons and their roles: \n- VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F}: Validate FNP lepton background estimates by probing lepton pT regimes and soft lepton requirements, while excluding SF-OS dilepton pairs. \n- CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}: Estimates t\\bar{t}Z background with specific MET and jet requirements. \n- CR^Z_{VV}: Estimates multi-boson background with MET and b-tagging constraints. \n- VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}: Validates t\\bar{t}Z background estimates. \n- VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,n-jet}} and VR_{VV}^{Z\\textrm{,}b\\textrm{-tag}}: Validate multi-boson background estimates, focusing on jet multiplicity and b-tagging.", "The 3ℓ (three-lepton) region requires at least three leptons, and its role is to target top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The 3ℓ SRs (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) require at least three leptons. Their roles include setting exclusion limits for BSM scenarios via upper limits on BSM events and cross-sections (via Tables 10 and 12), and enhancing SUSY model exclusion power through shape-fit analyses of kinematic distributions (as in Figures 6 and 9).", "The 3ℓ SRs (three-lepton signal regions) require at least three leptons. Their role is related to background estimation, specifically targeting backgrounds like $t\\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson processes.", "The 3ℓ SRs (three-lepton signal regions) require at least three leptons. Their role is to estimate Standard Model backgrounds (e.g., $t\\bar{t}Z$, multi-boson, fake/non-prompt leptons) by using control regions and Monte Carlo simulations for normalization and validation.", "The 3ℓ SRs require at least three leptons. Their role is implied to be signal regions where backgrounds (e.g., ttZ, multi-boson, fake/non-prompt leptons) are estimated, but the text does not explicitly describe their specific roles beyond their existence in the analysis.", "The 3ℓ region requires at least three leptons and is aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The 3ℓ region requires at least three leptons, and its role is to target top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The analysis distinguishes signals from the SM top quark pair production background by requiring either a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ decay or a pair of $b$ -tagged jets originating from the $h\to b\bar{b}$ decay, plus the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event.", "The presence of at least three signal leptons is required. The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes the SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays with each $\tilde{t}_{1}\rightarrow t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ . The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The region requiring at least three leptons uses an SF-OS lepton pair from $Z$ decay and an additional lepton from top quark decay.", "The region requiring three leptons is when an SF-OS lepton pair (from Z decay) and an additional lepton (from top quark decay) are present. The SF-OS pair identifies the Z boson decay, while the additional lepton helps distinguish the signal from background.", "The region that requires at least three leptons is referred to as the 3 ℓ selection, aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons.", "The regions SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ require at least three signal leptons. The requirement on the number of signal leptons makes these selections insensitive to potential contributions from alternative $\tilde{t}_{1}$ decays with each $\tilde{t}_{1}\rightarrow t\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ . The signal lepton multiplicity requirement depletes the contribution from the direct production of $\tilde{t}_{1}$ pairs to SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The regions VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ and VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ require at least three leptons and are used for probing the lepton $p_{\text{T}}$ regime and including soft lepton requirements, respectively. The control regions CR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ and CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ are used for the $t\bar{t}Z$ and multi-boson background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection. The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ region validates the $t\bar{t}Z$ background estimate, while the VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,}b\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, focusing on jet multiplicity and releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto, respectively.", "The regions VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ and VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ require at least three leptons. VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ probes the lepton $p_{\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$, while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$. The VR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ region primarily validates the $t\bar{t}Z$ background estimate. The VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,n-jet}}$ and VR ${}_{VV}^{Z\textrm{,}b\textrm{-tag}}$ regions validate the multi-boson background estimate, the former focusing on the extrapolation in jet multiplicity and the latter releasing the $b$ -tagged jet veto.", "The regions VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ and VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ require at least three leptons. VR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{F}}}$ probes the lepton $p_{\text{T}}$ regime in SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$, while VR ${}^{Z}_{2\textrm{F}}$ includes soft lepton requirements as in SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$. The control regions CR ${}^{Z}_{t\bar{t}Z}$ and CR ${}^{Z}_{VV}$ also require at least three leptons for the background estimation in the 3 $\\ell$ selection.", "The regions requiring at least three leptons are SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, and SR^Z_{2B}. Their roles are: SR^Z_{1A} targets large mass splittings in the first model using m^{3ℓ}_{T2}; SR^Z_{1B} targets small mass differences using p_T^{ℓℓ}; SR^Z_{2A} targets small mass splittings in the second model with upper pT bounds and no b-tag requirements; SR^Z_{2B} targets large mass splittings in the second model with b-tagged jets and high E_T^{miss}/p_T^{ℓℓ}.", "The regions requiring at least three leptons are the 3ℓ SRs (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}). Their roles involve contributing to exclusion limits for BSM/SUSY models via kinematic bins (e.g., E_T^miss, p_T^ℓℓ) to differentiate signal/background ratios, as detailed in Tables 10/12 and figures 6/8.", "The regions requiring at least three leptons are: (1) VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} (validating FNP lepton background estimates with criteria similar to signal regions but reduced prompt lepton contributions), (2) CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z} (estimating t\\bar{t}Z background), (3) CR^Z_{VV} (estimating multi-boson background), and (4) VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, and VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag} (validating t\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson background estimates via jet multiplicity/extrapolation checks).", "The regions requiring three leptons include those with an SF-OS lepton pair from Z decay and an additional lepton from top quark decay, or a b-tagged jet pair from Higgs decay plus an additional lepton. The SF-OS pair identifies the Z boson, the b-tagged jets identify the Higgs boson, and the additional lepton comes from top quark decay.", "The regions requiring ≥3 leptons are: (1) VR^Z_{1F} and VR^Z_{2F} (for validating fake/non-prompt lepton backgrounds), (2) CR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z} and CR^Z_{VV} (for estimating t\\bar{t}Z and multi-boson backgrounds), and (3) VR^Z_{t\\bar{t}Z}, VR_{VV}^{Z,n-jet}, and VR_{VV}^{Z,b-tag} (for validating those estimates).", "The regions that require at least three leptons are the 3 $\\ell$ SRs, which are used for model-dependent exclusion limits. The specific regions mentioned are SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$, SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$, and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$. The role of these regions is to provide observed and expected yields for the exclusion limits.", "The text describes two exclusive categories of events: one requiring at least three leptons (3 ℓ selection) aimed at top squark decays involving Z bosons, and another requiring exactly one lepton (1 ℓ selection) aimed at top squark decays involving Higgs bosons.", "The text mentions several regions (SRs) and their roles in the analysis, but it does not specify which regions require at least three leptons. The regions mentioned are SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{A}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{B}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}_{1{\textrm{AB}}}^{h}$ , SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$ , and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$ .", "The text mentions several signal regions (SRs) that require at least three leptons, including SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{A}}}$, SR ${}^{Z}_{1{\textrm{B}}}$, SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$, and SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{B}}}$.", "The text mentions specific signal regions (SRs) that require at least three leptons, including SR ${}^{Z}_{2{\textrm{A}}}$, and provides details about their roles in the analysis. These regions are used in exclusion fits and their observed and expected yields are shown in figures and tables.", "The text mentions the requirement of a same-flavour opposite-sign (SF-OS) lepton pair originating from the $Z\to\\ell^{+}\\ell^{-}$ decay and the presence of an additional lepton produced in the decay of the top quarks in the event. It also mentions the four-body decay $\tilde{t}_{1}\to bff^{\\prime}\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ where $f$ and $f^{\\prime}$ are two fermions from the $W^{*}$ decay, such as $\tilde{t}_{1}\to b\\ell\nu\\mathchoice{\\displaystyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\\displaystyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\textstyle\raise 1.72218pt\\hbox{$\textstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptstyle\raise 0.90417pt\\hbox{$\\scriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}{\\scriptscriptstyle\raise 0.64583pt\\hbox{$\\scriptscriptstyle\tilde{\\chi}^{0}_{1}$}}$ ."], "quote_supports": true, "support_why": "The cited sentences explicitly state that events are classified into a category requiring 'at least three leptons' (3ℓ selection) for Z boson-related top squark decays. Additionally, specific signal regions (SR^Z_{1A}, SR^Z_{1B}, SR^Z_{2A}, SR^Z_{2B}) are described with criteria that include at least three leptons. The roles of each region (targeting mass splittings, kinematic conditions like p_T bounds, b-tagged jets, etc.) are also detailed in the text. This directly answers the question about which regions require three leptons and their roles.", "judged_by": "Qwen/QwQ-32B-AWQ"}
